Exactly. The deadline made the interrogation necessary: we needed to explain what the evidence supported, what remained unresolved, and why. \'93We haven\'92t established incremental hedge value\'94 is a defensible result when we can show how we got there.\
There are four things I\'92d particularly like to investigate next. These are hypotheses to test, not conclusions from tonight.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Are we protecting the portfolio against the right event?
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Our whole-book downside metric and short-sleeve protection were answering different questions. I\'92d start by decomposing the mandate\'92s losses: broad equity moves, financial-sector moves, differences between the long and short businesses, and individual issuers. Then ask which component we actually want to insure. Perhaps the interesting problem is protecting a bank-versus-broker relative-value position, rather than buying generic protection against financial stocks falling.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Does protection arrive when it is usable?
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 This is the one that sparked my curiosity most. An option could provide valuable protection during a selloff, yet finish with little payoff after a rebound. Conversely, a good terminal result could conceal an uncomfortable financing path. I\'92d like to compare terminal wealth with 
\f2\i cash available during distress
\f0\i0 : peak financing needs, interim losses, and the ability to monetize protection. That would require a predetermined exit or monetization rule\'97not choosing the best sale date afterward.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can a useful risk forecast still produce an unattractive hedge?
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 We should separate predicting danger from deciding whether insurance is worth its price. With actual option quotes, we could test whether a forecast changes coverage usefully 
\f1\b after accounting for the price of protection
\f0\b0 . The interesting comparison would be adaptive versus static puts under the same constraints, alongside predetermined equity reduction. Better variance forecasts might help, do nothing, or encourage buying protection at unfavorable prices. Each outcome would teach us something.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Are our funding variables measuring the mechanism your intuition cares about?
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Deposits/liabilities was a tractable first proxy. I\'92d be much more interested in a narrowly specified vulnerability: runnable funding combined with asset losses, liquidity, or maturity mismatch. Your intuition could help identify the mechanism before we choose variables. Failed and acquired institutions would matter to that investigation; studying only surviving firms could leave out precisely the cases we want to understand.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 For the hands-on deep dive, I\'92d enjoy taking a few specific windows with you and reconstructing what the portfolio was exposed to, what the hedge could have done, and what information was available beforehand. Those case studies would generate hypotheses; we\'92d then freeze the tests separately.\
My strongest curiosity is that second question: 
\f1\b \strokec2 were we measuring insurance by its expiry payoff when the mandate actually needed liquidity along the way?
