\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Protection while holding:
\f0\b0  the puts\'92 increased value offsets some equity losses, cushioning portfolio NAV. You don\'92t have to sell them for that protection to exist.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Access to cash:
\f0\b0  selling some protection could realize gains to meet financing needs, rebalance, or purchase depressed assets. That requires an actual monetization rule\'97and executable prices.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Your suggestion of 
\f1\b \strokec2 selling the hedge during the crash and buying protection again later
\f0\b0 \strokec2  adds another strategy layer. It might help, but it introduces difficult decisions: when to sell, how much protection to retain, and when to re-enter. Selling before another leg down could leave the portfolio exposed; replacement protection could also be expensive. We\'92d need rules specified beforehand.\
Our study 
\f1\b \strokec2 did include intrawindow marks and drawdown measures
\f0\b0 \strokec2 , so we didn\'92t entirely overlook protection along the way. But terminal tail loss drove selection, and we didn\'92t test early monetization or demonstrate usable liquidity from real option quotes.\
So my question is narrower than \'93we evaluated it incorrectly\'94: 
\f1\b \strokec2 would a different protection objective reveal useful benefits that the terminal-loss objective doesn\'92t establish?
